<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laufilitonga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Laufilitonga was the last Tuʻi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, the sacred paramount chief of Tonga. During the civil wars of the early 19th century he was defeated in 1826 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">Tāufaʻāhau</a>, the future King George Tupou I, who went on to unite the islands under his own rule. He lived on with sacred rank but little power, and after his death in 1865 the ancient title of Tuʻi Tonga was not renewed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laufilitonga.jpg" alt="Laufilitonga" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tauʻolunga · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laufilitonga_cross.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fatafehi Laufilitonga was born on 24 August 1797, the eldest son of the Tuʻi Tonga Fatafehi Fuanunuʻiava and his wife Tupou Veiongo Moheofo. He was to be the 39th holder of the title of a dynasty that had ruled in Tonga since the days of the Tuʻi Tonga Empire. Little is known about his life. He succeeded his father in 1810 as head of the House of Tonga, but he was considered too young to be made Tuʻi Tonga, and by then the title had lost much of its power and prestige: real power lay with the Tuʻi Kanokupolu dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Laufilitonga hoped to restore the power of his line and tried to turn his role as spiritual leader into a political one. This brought him into conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">Tāufaʻāhau</a>, the chief who lived in the Haʻapai Islands and later became George Tupou I, the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>. The struggle was settled at the Battle of Velata, near Tongoleleka on the island of Lifuka, in 1826, where Laufilitonga was defeated; the chief of Haʻafeva, who had taken the side against him, was an important ally of his opponent.</p><p>He was installed as Tuʻi Tonga in 1827, but the office now brought him little real power. On 7 November 1851 he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and was baptised with the name Samuelio Fatafehi Laufilitonga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Laufilitonga died on 9 December 1865 and was buried in the langi, the royal tomb, called Tuʻofefafa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a>. No successor took the title: it was absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">George Tupou I</a>, who abolished it in 1875. His widow, Sālote Lupepauʻu, later married George Tupou I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Laufilitonga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Out of them rose Tāufaʻāhau, born around 1797, who inherited a contested position in the Haʻapai islands in 1820 and defeated <b>Laufilitonga</b>, heir to the Tuʻi Tonga title, at the Battle of Velata on Lifuka in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1826 <b>Laufilitonga</b>, heir to the title, was defeated at the Battle of Velata on Lifuka by Tāufaʻāhau, the rising leader of Haʻapai; the chiefs of Tongatapu installed Laufilitonga as Tuʻi Tonga in 1827, but he never regained real power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
